use web
use json
into editor.he
lang he

```he
use core
use md
```

The module is namespace `editor`. It owns the editor column of the Amni-Code page: the Monaco editor (a JavaScript library that is driven only through the web module's js calls), its tabs, saving, the AI suggestion, the command palette, quick open, the status bar, the breadcrumbs and the file change stream. It calls only `web`, `json`, `core`, `md` and the standard library, never another feature module. Text is a growable byte buffer. Page elements are found by id with `web::el`. A handle the host makes for something short lived (a list item, an event target, a Monaco position, uri or model that is no longer needed) is given back with `web::release` as soon as it has been read. A JSON string inside a JSON argument is always written with `json::format_string`.

The shared functions it uses are all written with the out buffer first: `core::post` takes an action name and an argument and queues the action; `core::toast` takes the message; `core::t` appends the translation of a key to an out buffer; `core::sse_take` takes the stream buffer, an event buffer and a data buffer and gives whether it pulled one complete event; `core::url_part(out, text)` appends text percent-encoded like JavaScript's encodeURIComponent; `md::esc` and `md::esc_html` append the escaped text to an out buffer. Both modules are imported with use lines at the top of the file.

Tags are i32 constants in 400 to 499. `T_TOGGLE` is 400 (click on the element "editor-toggle"), `T_BOOT` 401 (a timer), `T_NOP` 402 (tags js calls whose result nobody reads), `T_LOADED` 403 (callback: Monaco's main module has loaded), `T_CREATED` 404 (result of creating the editor), `T_SAVE_KEY` 405 and `T_SUGGEST_KEY` 406 (editor key callbacks), `T_CURSOR` 407 (cursor callback), `T_POSITION` 408, `T_URI` 409, `T_FIND_MODEL` 410, `T_MAKE_MODEL` 411, `T_BLANK_MODEL` 412, `T_VIEW_STATE` 413, `T_SAVE_VALUE` 414, `T_SUGGEST_POS` 415 and `T_SUGGEST_VALUE` 416 (js call results), `T_OPEN_FETCH` 417, `T_SAVE_FETCH` 418 and `T_QO_FETCH` 419 (fetches), `T_FS_STREAM` 420 (the file change stream) and `T_FS_RETRY` 421 (its restart timer), `T_CMD_INPUT` 422, `T_CMD_KEY` 423, `T_CMD_HOVER` 424, `T_CMD_OVERLAY` 425 and `T_CMD_FOCUS` 426 (command palette), `T_QO_INPUT` 427, `T_QO_KEY` 428, `T_QO_HOVER` 429, `T_QO_OVERLAY` 430 and `T_QO_FOCUS` 431 (quick open), and `T_DOC_KEY` 432 (keydown on the document).

A line list is a byte buffer with one entry per line, every entry ended by a newline. `line_count(list)` is the number of entries. `line_at(list, n, out)` appends entry n to out and gives whether entry n exists. `line_index(list, entry)` is the position of the first entry equal to the text entry, or minus 1. `line_add(list, entry)` appends an entry. `line_put(list, n, entry)` replaces entry n and `line_drop(list, n)` removes it, both by rebuilding the list through a temporary buffer.

Tab state. `tab_paths` is a module line list of the file paths of the open tabs. `tab_states` is a module line list with one entry per tab holding that tab's saved Monaco view state as JSON text, "null" when it has none. `tab_models` is a module i32 array with the Monaco model handle of each tab, 0 while the tab has no model. The three always have the same length. `active` is the index of the active tab, minus 1 at first. `editor_h` is the handle of the Monaco editor, 0 until it exists, and `booting` says that loading Monaco has started. `vs_path` is the path whose view state a pending saveViewState call belongs to.

`base_name(path, out)` appends the part of path after its last forward slash or backslash, the whole path when it has none.

`current_file(out)` appends the path of the active tab and gives true, or gives false when `active` is not a valid tab index.

`sidebar_base(out)` appends the text of the first element with the class "sb-path" inside the element "sidebar-tree" (the settings module renders the sidebar's directory there), or nothing when there is none, and releases that element's handle.

`language_of(path, out)` appends the Monaco language of a file path. Take the text after the last "." of the path, or the whole path when it has no ".", in lower case (ASCII), and map it: py to python, js to javascript, ts to typescript, jsx to javascriptreact, tsx to typescriptreact, rs to rust, html to html, css to css, json to json, md to markdown, sh to bash, bat to bat, toml to ini, yaml to yaml, yml to yaml, xml to xml, sql to sql, c to c, cpp to cpp, h to c, hpp to cpp, java to java, go to go, rb to ruby, php to php, swift to swift, kt to kotlin, r to r, lua to lua, zig to zig, and anything else to plaintext.

`render_tabs()` does nothing when the element "tab-bar" is missing. Otherwise it sets that element's html to one div per open tab, in order. The div has the class "tab", followed by " active" for the active tab, the attribute data-act "editor.tab", data-arg the tab's index, and title the full path escaped with `md::esc`. Inside it come a span with the file name (`base_name` of the path) escaped with `md::esc_html`, then a span with the class "tab-close", data-act "editor.tab_close" and the same data-arg, holding the character "✕".

`render_crumbs(path)` fills the element "breadcrumbs". It does nothing when that element is missing, and with an empty path it only empties the element's html. Otherwise it splits the path at every forward slash and backslash, drops the empty parts, and sets the html to one span per part in order, each part escaped with `md::esc_html`. Before every part except the first goes the span `<span class="bc-sep">›</span>`, and the span of the last part is `<span style="color:var(--accent)">` while the other spans are plain `<span>`.

`set_file_label(path)` sets the text of the element "editor-filename" to `base_name` of path.

`update_status()` does nothing without a Monaco editor. Otherwise it calls the editor's "getPosition" method with tag `T_POSITION`, and when `current_file` has a file it sets the text of the element "sb-lang" to `language_of` that file with its first letter in upper case.

`on_position()` handles `T_POSITION`. It takes the handle `web::js_handle` gives for `web::ev_id()`. When that is not 0 it sets the text of the element "sb-pos" to "Ln ", the handle's number "lineNumber", ", Col " and its number "column" (read with `web::get_num`, as whole numbers), then releases the handle.

`toggle()` flips the class "open" of the element "editor-col". When it is open afterwards it adds the class "active" to the element "editor-toggle" and, when there is no Monaco editor yet, starts the timer `T_BOOT` of 300 ms; when it is closed afterwards it removes the class "active" from "editor-toggle". `ensure_open()` calls `toggle` only when "editor-col" does not have the class "open".

`boot()` handles `T_BOOT`. It does nothing when `editor_h` is not 0 or `booting` is true. Otherwise it sets `booting` and takes the page global "require" with `web::js_global`. It calls that handle's "config" method with the JSON array of one object whose member "paths" is an object whose member "vs" is "/vendor/monaco/vs", with tag `T_NOP`. Then it calls the handle itself, with an empty method name, with the JSON array of two elements: an array of the one string "vs/editor/editor.main", and an object whose member "$cb" is the number `T_LOADED`; the call's tag is `T_NOP`.

`on_loaded()` handles `T_LOADED`. The theme is "vs" when the body has the attribute "data-theme" equal to "light", otherwise "vs-dark". It calls the "create" method of the page global "monaco.editor" with the JSON array of two elements, tag `T_CREATED`: an object whose member "$h" is the handle of the element "monaco-container", and the options object with value "" (empty string), language "plaintext", the theme, automaticLayout true, fontSize 14, minimap an object with enabled false, and scrollBeyondLastLine false.

`on_created()` handles `T_CREATED`. It sets `editor_h` to what `web::js_handle` gives for `web::ev_id()`; when that is 0 it clears `booting` and stops. Otherwise it reads the numbers "CtrlCmd" of the page global "monaco.KeyMod" and "KeyS" and "Space" of "monaco.KeyCode" with `web::get_num` and calls the editor's "addCommand" method twice, with tag `T_NOP`: with the JSON array of the whole number CtrlCmd bitwise-or KeyS and an object whose "$cb" is `T_SAVE_KEY`, and with CtrlCmd bitwise-or Space and `T_SUGGEST_KEY`. It calls the editor's "onDidChangeCursorPosition" with the JSON array of one object whose "$cb" is `T_CURSOR`, tag `T_NOP`. When at least one tab is open it calls `show_tab` with `active`, or with 0 when `active` is negative. Finally it calls `continue_open`.

`show_tab(idx)` does nothing when idx is outside the tab list. When there is an editor and `active` is a valid tab index, it first remembers that tab's path in `vs_path` and calls the editor's "saveViewState" method with tag `T_VIEW_STATE`. Then `active` becomes idx and `set_file_label` is called with the tab's path. When there is an editor: when the tab's model is not 0 it calls the editor's "setModel" with the JSON array of the object whose "$h" is that model; when the tab's saved state is not "null" it calls "restoreViewState" with the JSON array holding that saved JSON text itself as its one element; then it calls "focus" and `update_status`. All those calls use tag `T_NOP`. Finally it calls `render_crumbs` with the tab's path and `render_tabs`.

`on_view_state()` handles `T_VIEW_STATE`. When the call succeeded it reads the result JSON text with `web::js_result` and stores it with `line_put` in `tab_states` at the position of `vs_path` in `tab_paths`, when `vs_path` is there.

`dispose_model(h)` calls the method "dispose" of the handle h with an empty JSON array, tag `T_NOP`, and releases h.

`close_tab(idx)` does nothing when idx is outside the tab list. It calls `dispose_model` with the tab's model when that is not 0, then removes the tab from `tab_paths`, `tab_states` and `tab_models`. When no tab is left, `active` becomes minus 1, the file label text becomes "untitled.py", and when there is an editor it calls the "createModel" method of the page global "monaco.editor" with the JSON array of the two strings "" (empty) and "plaintext", tag `T_BLANK_MODEL`. When tabs are left, `active` becomes the smaller of idx and the last tab index, and `show_tab` is called with it. In every case it ends with `render_tabs`.

`on_blank_model()` handles `T_BLANK_MODEL`. When `web::js_handle` of the event id is not 0 it calls the editor's "setModel" with the JSON array of the object whose "$h" is that handle, tag `T_NOP`, and then releases the handle.

Opening a file. `open_q` is a module line list of paths waiting to be opened. `open_busy` is true while one file is going through the steps below, `open_cur` is its path, `open_text` its content, `open_uri` the handle of its Monaco uri (0 when none) and `await_editor` says that it waits for the editor to exist.

`queue_open(path)` adds the path to `open_q` with `line_add` and calls `next_open`.

`next_open()` does nothing while `open_busy` is true or `open_q` is empty. Otherwise it moves the first entry of `open_q` into `open_cur` (and drops it from the list), sets `open_busy` and fetches "/api/read_file?path=" followed by the path appended with `core::url_part`, with `web::fetch` and tag `T_OPEN_FETCH`.

`on_open_fetch()` handles `T_OPEN_FETCH`. It takes the body with `web::fetch_take` of the event id and then `web::fetch_free`. When the HTTP status is not 0 and the body parses as JSON that is not null, with `json::parse` into a local `json::Doc`, the content is the string member "content" of the root, empty text when it is missing or not a string, and it calls `load` with `open_cur` and the content. Otherwise it posts the action "chat.system" with `core::post`, with the argument "Could not open: " followed by `open_cur`, and calls `finish_open`.

`load(path, content)` copies content into `open_text`. When `line_index` finds path in `tab_paths` it calls `show_tab` with that index and `ensure_open`, then `finish_open`. Otherwise, when there is an editor, it clears `vs_path`, and when `current_file` fills `vs_path` it calls the editor's "saveViewState" with "[]" and tag `T_VIEW_STATE`, so the tab being left keeps its cursor; then it adds the tab with `line_add` to `tab_paths` (path) and `tab_states` ("null") and pushes 0 to `tab_models`, sets `active` to the new last index, calls `set_file_label` with the path, `render_crumbs`, `render_tabs` and `ensure_open`; then it calls `start_model` when there is an editor, and otherwise sets `await_editor`.

`continue_open()` calls `start_model` and clears `await_editor` when `await_editor` is true and there is an editor.

`start_model()` calls the "Uri.parse" method of the page global "monaco" with the JSON array of the one string "file:///" followed by `open_cur` with every backslash replaced by a forward slash and every leading forward slash dropped (a Linux path such as /work/a.py gives file:///work/a.py, because Uri.parse throws on four slashes), tag `T_URI`.

`on_uri()` handles `T_URI`. It sets `open_uri` to `web::js_handle` of the event id; when that is 0 it calls `finish_open`, otherwise it calls the "getModel" method of the page global "monaco.editor" with the JSON array of the object whose "$h" is `open_uri`, tag `T_FIND_MODEL`.

`on_find_model()` handles `T_FIND_MODEL`. When `web::js_handle` of the event id is not 0 it calls `attach` with that handle. Otherwise it calls the "createModel" method of the page global "monaco.editor" with the JSON array of three elements, tag `T_MAKE_MODEL`: `open_text` as a JSON string, the `language_of` `open_cur` as a JSON string, and the object whose "$h" is `open_uri`.

`on_make_model()` handles `T_MAKE_MODEL`. It calls `attach` with `web::js_handle` of the event id when that is not 0, and `finish_open` otherwise.

`attach(h)` finds the position of `open_cur` in `tab_paths`. When the tab is gone it calls `dispose_model` with h. Otherwise it stores h as that tab's entry of `tab_models`, and when that tab is the active one and there is an editor it calls the editor's "setModel" with the JSON array of the object whose "$h" is h, tag `T_NOP`, and then calls `update_status` so the status bar shows the new model's language; it then calls `render_tabs`. In both cases it ends with `finish_open`.

`finish_open()` releases `open_uri` when it is not 0 and sets it to 0, clears `open_text`, sets `open_busy` and `await_editor` to false and calls `next_open`.

`save()` shows a window alert when there is no editor or `current_file` has no file: it calls the method "alert" of the web module's window handle with the JSON array of the string "Open a file first!", tag `T_NOP`, and stops. Otherwise it keeps the current file in the module buffer `save_path` and calls the editor's "getValue" method with tag `T_SAVE_VALUE`.

`on_save_value()` handles `T_SAVE_VALUE`. It reads the result JSON text with `web::js_result`, parses it with `json::parse` into a local `json::Doc` and takes the root as a string, the content. It then sends `web::request` with the url "/api/write_file", the method "POST", the headers JSON `{"Content-Type":"application/json"}`, the body `{"path":` `save_path` as a JSON string `,"content":` the content as a JSON string `}`, tag `T_SAVE_FETCH` and timeout 0.

`on_save_fetch()` handles `T_SAVE_FETCH`. It takes the status with `web::ev_status` and calls `web::fetch_free` with the event id. When the status is 0 it posts "chat.system" with the argument "Save failed - check connection.". Otherwise (whatever the status) it posts "chat.system" with the argument "Saved ", `save_path` and " from editor!", and then shows the toast "Saved " followed by `base_name` of `save_path` with `core::toast`.

`suggest()` does nothing when there is no editor or `current_file` has no file. Otherwise it keeps the current file in the module buffer `sug_path` and calls the editor's "getPosition" with tag `T_SUGGEST_POS`.

`on_suggest_pos()` handles `T_SUGGEST_POS`. It keeps the number "lineNumber" of the handle `web::js_handle` gives for the event id (as a whole number, in the module integer `sug_line`, 0 when the handle is 0), releases that handle when it is not 0, and calls the editor's "getValue" with tag `T_SUGGEST_VALUE`.

`on_suggest_value()` handles `T_SUGGEST_VALUE`. It reads the result JSON text with `web::js_result`, parses it with `json::parse` into a local `json::Doc` and takes the root as a string, the content. The prompt is "AI suggestion/completion for ", `sug_path`, " around line ", `sug_line`, a colon, a newline, a line of three backtick characters, a newline, the content, a newline, a line of three backtick characters and a final newline. It sets the "value" property of the element "input" to the prompt with `web::prop`, calls `web::call` with the element "send" and "click", then calls `toggle`.

`COMMANDS()` is the text of the palette commands, one command per line, each line made of four fields separated by "|": the label, the key hint, the action to post and the action's argument. A label that starts with "%" is not shown as it is: the text after the "%" is a translation key and `core::t` gives the label.
```he
COMMANDS() const byte[:]: "New Chat||chat.new|\nToggle Editor|Ctrl+E|editor.toggle|\nToggle Diff Panel||chat.diff|\nSave File|Ctrl+S|editor.save|\nQuick Open File|Ctrl+P|editor.quick_open|\nToggle Settings||settings.open|\nToggle Theme||look.theme_toggle|\nCycle Accent Color||look.accent|\nChat History||chat.history|\nAI Suggestion|Ctrl+Space|editor.suggest|\nClose Active Tab||editor.close_tab|\nNext Tab||editor.next_tab|\nPrevious Tab||editor.prev_tab|\nFocus Chat Input||chat.focus|\nUndo Changes||chat.undo|\nAccept Changes||chat.accept|\nOpen Amni-AI (Adam) ↗||look.amni_ai|\nSkin: Braid||look.theme|braid\nSkin: Amniscient||look.theme|amniscient\nTheme: Jarvis||look.theme|jarvis\nTheme: Scient||look.theme|scient\nTheme: Clean Dark||look.theme|clean-dark\nTheme: Clean Light||look.theme|clean-light\nTheme: Nebula||look.theme|nebula\nTheme: Terminal||look.theme|terminal\nTheme: Solar||look.theme|solar\nCycle Amni-scient Theme||look.theme_cycle|\n%split_view||panes.create|"
```

`command_field(n, k, out)` appends field k (0 label, 1 key hint, 2 action, 3 argument) of line n of `COMMANDS` to out, and gives whether line n exists. For field 0, a label that starts with "%" is replaced by `core::t` of the key after the "%".

`cmd_hits` is a module int array and `cmd_sel` a module integer that starts at 0. `match_commands(query)` clears `cmd_hits` and pushes the number of every command line whose label (as `command_field` gives it) contains the query, both compared in lower case (ASCII); an empty query matches every line.

`render_commands(query)` calls `match_commands`, limits `cmd_sel` to the smaller of itself and the larger of the hit count minus 1 and 0, and sets the html of the element "cmd-list" to one div per hit. The div has the class "cmd-item", followed by " selected" for the hit at position `cmd_sel`, the attribute data-idx the hit's position in the hits, data-act "editor.cmd" and data-arg the command's line number. Inside is a span with the label escaped with `md::esc_html`, followed, when the key hint is not empty, by a span with the class "cmd-key" holding the key hint as it is.

`open_palette()` adds the class "open" to the elements "cmd-overlay" and "cmd-palette", sets the "value" property of "cmd-input" to the empty text, sets `cmd_sel` to 0, calls `render_commands` with the empty text and starts the timer `T_CMD_FOCUS` of 50 ms. `close_palette()` removes the class "open" from "cmd-overlay" and "cmd-palette".

`run_command(n)` calls `close_palette`, then, when line n of `COMMANDS` exists, posts its action (field 2) with its argument (field 3) using `core::post`.

`hovered_index(selector)` gives the number in the attribute "data-idx" of the closest element matching the selector, searched upward from `web::ev_target()` with `web::closest`, or minus 1 when there is none or the attribute is missing. When it found an element it releases it and, when the target is a different handle, the target as well.

`on_cmd_key()` handles `T_CMD_KEY`, the keydown in the element "cmd-input". It reads the key with `web::ev_key` and the input's value with `web::ev_value`, and calls `match_commands` with that value. For "ArrowDown" it prevents the default with `web::ev_prevent`, makes `cmd_sel` the smaller of `cmd_sel` plus 1 and the hit count minus 1, and calls `render_commands` with the value. For "ArrowUp" it prevents the default, makes `cmd_sel` the larger of `cmd_sel` minus 1 and 0, and calls `render_commands` with the value. For "Enter" with at least one hit it prevents the default and calls `run_command` with the hit at position `cmd_sel`. For "Escape" it calls `close_palette`.

Quick open. `qo_names` is a module byte buffer with the file names of the last listing one after another and `qo_ends` a module int array with the end offset of each name in it. `qo_prefix` is a module byte buffer: the directory the listing was made for, plus the separator. `qo_hits` is a module int array and `qo_sel` a module integer that starts at 0.

`open_quick_open()` fetches "/api/ls?path=" followed by what `sidebar_base` gives appended with `core::url_part`, with `web::fetch` and tag `T_QO_FETCH`.

`on_qo_fetch()` handles `T_QO_FETCH`. It takes the body with `web::fetch_take` of the event id and then `web::fetch_free`, and clears `qo_names` and `qo_ends`. The base is what `sidebar_base` gives with every trailing forward slash and backslash removed; `qo_prefix` becomes the base followed by "/" when the base contains a "/" and by a backslash otherwise. When the status is not 0 and the body parses as JSON (`json::parse` into a local `json::Doc`) with an array member "files", every string element of it is appended to `qo_names`, with its end offset pushed to `qo_ends`. In every case it then adds the class "open" to the elements "qo-overlay" and "qo-palette", sets the "value" property of "qo-input" to the empty text, sets `qo_sel` to 0, calls `render_files` with the empty text and starts the timer `T_QO_FOCUS` of 50 ms.

`match_files(query)` clears `qo_hits` and pushes the number of every name in `qo_names` that contains the query, both compared in lower case (ASCII); an empty query matches every name.

`render_files(query)` calls `match_files`, limits `qo_sel` to the smaller of itself and the larger of the hit count minus 1 and 0, and sets the html of the element "qo-list" to one div for each of the first 30 hits. The div has the class "qo-item", followed by " selected" for the hit at position `qo_sel`, the attribute data-idx the hit's position in the hits, data-act "editor.qo_open" and data-arg the path, which is `qo_prefix` followed by the name, escaped with `md::esc`. Inside are a span with the name escaped with `md::esc_html` and a span with the class "qo-path" holding the path escaped with `md::esc_html`.

`close_quick_open()` removes the class "open" from the elements "qo-overlay" and "qo-palette".

`on_qo_key()` handles `T_QO_KEY`, the keydown in the element "qo-input". It reads the key with `web::ev_key` and the input's value with `web::ev_value`, and calls `match_files` with that value. For "ArrowDown" it prevents the default with `web::ev_prevent`, makes `qo_sel` the smaller of `qo_sel` plus 1, the hit count minus 1 and 29, and calls `render_files` with the value. For "ArrowUp" it prevents the default, makes `qo_sel` the larger of `qo_sel` minus 1 and 0, and calls `render_files` with the value. For "Enter" with at least one hit it prevents the default, calls `close_quick_open`, and calls `queue_open` with `qo_prefix` followed by the hit at position `qo_sel`. For "Escape" it calls `close_quick_open`.

`on_doc_key()` handles `T_DOC_KEY`, the keydown on the document. It reads the key with `web::ev_key` and the modifier bits with `web::ev_mods`; "mod" means control or meta held. With mod and shift held and the key exactly "P" it prevents the default and calls `open_palette`; otherwise with mod held, shift not held and the key exactly "p" it prevents the default and calls `open_quick_open`. Separately, for the key "Escape" it calls `close_palette` when the element "cmd-palette" has the class "open" and `close_quick_open` when the element "qo-palette" has the class "open".

The file change stream. `fs_buf` is a module byte buffer. `start_fs()` clears it and starts `web::stream` of "/api/fs-events" with the method "GET", the headers JSON `{"Accept":"text/event-stream"}`, an empty body and tag `T_FS_STREAM`.

`on_fs(kind)` handles `T_FS_STREAM`. For `web::EV_CHUNK` it takes the chunk with `web::fetch_take` of the event id into a temporary buffer, calls `web::fetch_free`, appends it to `fs_buf`, then repeats `core::sse_take` on `fs_buf` and for every event named "fs" posts the action "settings.sidebar_refresh" with the empty argument using `core::post`. For `web::EV_FETCH` (the stream ended) it calls `web::fetch_free` with the event id and starts the timer `T_FS_RETRY` of 3000 ms. Other kinds are ignored.

`init()` registers the listeners: click on "editor-toggle" with `T_TOGGLE`; "input" on "cmd-input" with `T_CMD_INPUT`; "keydown" on "cmd-input" with `T_CMD_KEY`; "mouseover" on "cmd-list" with `T_CMD_HOVER`; click on "cmd-overlay" with `T_CMD_OVERLAY`; the same four on "qo-input" (`T_QO_INPUT`, `T_QO_KEY`), "qo-list" (`T_QO_HOVER`) and "qo-overlay" (`T_QO_OVERLAY`); and "keydown" on the web module's document with `T_DOC_KEY`; all with no flags. It then calls `start_fs`.

`on_cmd_input()` sets `cmd_sel` to 0 and calls `render_commands` with the event's value read with `web::ev_value`. `on_qo_input()` does the same with `qo_sel` and `render_files`.

`on_cmd_hover()` takes `hovered_index` with the selector ".cmd-item". When that is not minus 1 and differs from `cmd_sel`, it makes it the new `cmd_sel` and calls `render_commands` with the "value" property of the element "cmd-input" (read with `web::get_prop`). `on_qo_hover()` does the same with the selector ".qo-item", `qo_sel`, `render_files` and the element "qo-input".

`event(tag, kind)` is called by the root for every tag from 400 to 499 and gives nothing. `T_TOGGLE` calls `toggle`; `T_BOOT` calls `boot`; `T_LOADED` calls `on_loaded`; `T_CREATED` calls `on_created`; `T_SAVE_KEY` calls `save`; `T_SUGGEST_KEY` calls `suggest`; `T_CURSOR` calls `update_status`; `T_POSITION` calls `on_position`; `T_URI` calls `on_uri`; `T_FIND_MODEL` calls `on_find_model`; `T_MAKE_MODEL` calls `on_make_model`; `T_BLANK_MODEL` calls `on_blank_model`; `T_VIEW_STATE` calls `on_view_state`; `T_SAVE_VALUE` calls `on_save_value`; `T_SUGGEST_POS` calls `on_suggest_pos`; `T_SUGGEST_VALUE` calls `on_suggest_value`; `T_OPEN_FETCH` calls `on_open_fetch`, `T_SAVE_FETCH` calls `on_save_fetch` and `T_QO_FETCH` calls `on_qo_fetch`, each only when the kind is `web::EV_FETCH` (the fetch also reports progress with another kind); `T_FS_STREAM` calls `on_fs` with the kind; `T_FS_RETRY` calls `start_fs`; `T_CMD_INPUT` calls `on_cmd_input`; `T_CMD_KEY` calls `on_cmd_key`; `T_CMD_HOVER` calls `on_cmd_hover`; `T_CMD_OVERLAY` calls `close_palette`; `T_CMD_FOCUS` calls `web::call` with the element "cmd-input" and "focus"; `T_QO_INPUT` calls `on_qo_input`; `T_QO_KEY` calls `on_qo_key`; `T_QO_HOVER` calls `on_qo_hover`; `T_QO_OVERLAY` calls `close_quick_open`; `T_QO_FOCUS` calls `web::call` with the element "qo-input" and "focus"; `T_DOC_KEY` calls `on_doc_key`. Other tags are ignored.

`act(verb, arg)` is called by the root for every queued action named "editor." followed by the verb. By verb: "toggle" calls `toggle`; "save" calls `save`; "suggest" calls `suggest`; "palette" calls `open_palette`; "quick_open" calls `open_quick_open`; "open" calls `queue_open` with arg; "tab" calls `show_tab` with arg as a whole number; "tab_close" calls `close_tab` with arg as a whole number; "close_tab" calls `close_tab` with `active` when `active` is not negative; "next_tab" calls `show_tab` with `active` plus 1 modulo the tab count when there is more than one tab; "prev_tab" calls `show_tab` with `active` minus 1 plus the tab count, modulo the tab count, when there is more than one tab; "cmd" calls `run_command` with arg as a whole number; "qo_open" calls `close_quick_open` and then `queue_open` with arg. Other verbs are ignored.
